{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ yogurt yoghurt) \"type 2 diabetes\" \"dose-response\" \"meta-analysis\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_666414104#5_1372961873","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q89","rank":2,"snippet":"Type 2 Diabetes Highlights Synopsis The Evidence Potential Mechanisms Risk factors Milk proteins Dairy fatty acids Other dairy components Conclusion References Keywords \" , \"segment\": \"Whole milk consumption was associated with a 13% decreased risk of type 2 diabetes, compared to low dairy fat intake (relative risk of 0.87, 95% CI: 0.78–0.96). A <cover>dose-response meta-analysis published by Gijsbers et al. in 2016 examined the associations between dairy foods and the development of type 2 diabetes. This comprehensive analysis included data from 22 prospective cohort studies involving adults who were healthy at baseline (N=579,832). 6 Each added 200 g/day serving of total dairy foods was associated with a 3% reduced risk of type 2 diabetes (relative risk of 0.97, 95% CI: 0.95-1.0). Yogurt </cover>consumption of 80 g/day was associated with a 14% decreased risk of type 2 diabetes, compared to 0g/day (relative risk of 0.86, 95% CI: 0.83-0.90). A systematic review of 21 meta-analyses of prospective cohort studies published by Drouin-Chartier et al.\" , \"start_char\": 2972.000000 , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_666414104#4_1372960295","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
