{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1182911155#17_2150181762","relevance":2,"reason":"This Greek yogurt nutrition/benefits article is dedicated to Greek yogurt and summarizes systematic-review evidence on cardiovascular outcomes, including neutral and favorable findings, but it is a consumer-style overview rather than a dedicated scientific evidence synthesis.","learned":"Systematic reviews cited in a Greek yogurt article report neutral or favorable cardiovascular evidence: no association with cardiovascular/stroke risk in one 18-study review, and lower cardiovascular disease risk with >200 g/day yogurt in a nine-study review."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"yogurt consumption\" cardiovascular disease \"systematic review\")","reason":"Search for the underlying dedicated systematic-review evidence on yogurt consumption and cardiovascular disease risk, prompted by the promising cardiovascular evidence in the latest result."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"yogurt consumption\" cardiovascular disease \"systematic review\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1182911155#16_2150179576","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q137","rank":1,"snippet":"properties. Dietary protein is widely thought to be the most satiating of the three macronutrients ( 21 ). Key Point: Greek yogurt is an ideal high-protein snack, and it leads to reduced hunger compared to lower protein snack options. 7) Potential Heart Benefits Numerous systematic reviews and meta-analyses suggest that fermented dairy products, such as Greek yogurt, may be good for the heart; A 2016 systematic review on dairy product consumption and the risk of cardiovascular disease demonstrated a favorable impact of fermented dairy on stroke risk and hypertension. Additionally, there was a neutral impact on cardiovascular risk ( 22 ). In a systematic review of 18 studies that followed 762,414 individuals for 8 to 26 years, <cover>yogurt consumption had no association with cardiovascular or stroke risk ( 23 ). In a systematic review of nine studies featuring 291,236 participants, a daily serving of >200 g yogurt was significantly associated with a lower risk of cardiovascular disease ( </cover>24 ).\" , \"start_char\": 8685.000000 , \"title\": \"Greek Yogurt 101: Nutrition Facts and Health Benefits | Nutrition Advance\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nutritionadvance.com/greek-yogurt-nutrition-benefits/\" }"}}
